<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A selective discussion of the existing architectural choices and design of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> is provided in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Main Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin this section by presenting a general unsupervised training cost for  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VINA</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VINA</abbr>) in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Variational () ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. As mentioned earlier, this formulation unifies several commonly used cost functions in the literature. For simplicity we focus our presentation in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Variational () ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> for the case of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr>, but we then illustrate how it naturally applies to <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">NF</abbr> in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Variational  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">USL</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">USL</abbr>)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main methodological contribution in this paper begins with the observation, also used in priors works such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nowozin et al. (2016)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grover et al. (2018)</a></cite>, that several commonly used statistical divergence measures used in generative modeling admit variational representations. More specifically, with <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{x}}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}}\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{z}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{x}}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}}\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{z}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting an <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr>, we wish to minimize some divergence of the form</p>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D_{\phi}(P_{Y,Z},P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})=\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{\phi}}\mathbb{E}\left[\phi(g,X,Y,Z,T)\right],\quad\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{emp. loss}}}{{\implies}}\quad\hat{L}=\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{\phi}}\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\phi\left(g,X_{i},Y_{i},Z_{i},T\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.889em">,</mo><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mtext>emp. loss</mtext></mover><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{\phi}(P_{Y,Z},P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})=\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{\phi}}\mathbb{E}\left[\phi(g,X,Y,Z,T)\right],\quad\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{emp. loss}}}{{\implies}}\quad\hat{L}=\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{\phi}}\;\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\phi\left(g,X_{i},Y_{i},Z_{i},T\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes some class of functions, usually from <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we will refer to it as the “critic class” following the convention used in the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">GAN</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">GAN</abbr>) literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goodfellow et al., 2014</a>)</cite>. In practice, this class can be represented by machine learning models such as neural networks, or more analytically tractable classes such as <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RKHS</abbr>. Our theoretical results in the next two subsections explore the trade-offs involved in choosing more expressive <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and approximation guarantees.
For different choices of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\phi,\mathcal{G}_{\phi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\phi,\mathcal{G}_{\phi})</annotation></semantics></math>, the above formulation recovers various popular loss functions such as relative entropy (and more generally, the <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence family), kernel-<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MMD</abbr> (and more generally, the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">IPM</abbr> family), energy distance, and the Wasserstein metric. Besides the conceptual unification, this approach also allows us to obtain theoretical guarantees on the approximation performance of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> trained via empirical risk minimization strategy, as we discuss in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> for <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> (and in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> for <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">NF</abbr>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Before proceeding further, we recall two important family of distance or divergence measures that are realizations of (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.1 Variational () ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). The first class of distance metrics are the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">IPM</abbr>, which for some function class <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> that is closed under negation (i.e., if <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, then so does <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-g</annotation></semantics></math>), are defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{IPM}(P_{Y,Z},P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})=\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\mathbb{E}\left[g(Y,Z)-g(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X))\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>IPM</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{IPM}(P_{Y,Z},P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})=\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\mathbb{E}\left[g(Y,Z)-g(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X))\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which corresponds to <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{\mathrm{IPM}}(g,x,y,z,T)=g(y,z)-g(y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>IPM</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{\mathrm{IPM}}(g,x,y,z,T)=g(y,z)-g(y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Perhaps the most important element of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">IPM</abbr> family is the kernel-<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MMD</abbr> metric, where the critic class (also known as the witness class) is the unit norm ball <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RKHS</abbr> associated with a positive-definite kernel <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. An immediate consequence of the reproducing property of such <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is that for square integrable kernels, we have</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{MMD}(P_{Y,Z},P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})=\|\mu_{P_{Y,Z}}-\mu_{P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)}}\|_{k},\quad\text{where}\quad\mu_{P}=\int k(x,\cdot)dP(x)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>MMD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>where</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{MMD}(P_{Y,Z},P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})=\|\mu_{P_{Y,Z}}-\mu_{P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)}}\|_{k},\quad\text{where}\quad\mu_{P}=\int k(x,\cdot)dP(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">is referred to as the kernel mean-embedding of a distribution <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RKHS</abbr> norm, and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∫</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int</annotation></semantics></math> above is a Bochner integral. This leads to a very natural interpretation of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MMD</abbr> metric between two distributions <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>: it is the distance (as measured by the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RKHS</abbr> norm) between the two representative elements <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{P}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>Q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> associated with the two distributions. Other important <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">IPM</abbr> include the total variation distance, and the Wasserstein <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> metric (also known as the earth-mover distance), which by using Kantorovich-Rubinstein duality (Theorem 1.14 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Villani (2021)</a></cite>, and Theorem 11.8.2. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dudley (2018)</a></cite>) can be represented as an <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">IPM</abbr> associated with the class <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz functions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The second important class of divergence measures captured by (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.1 Variational () ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is the family of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergences. For any convex, lower semicontinuous <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:(0,\infty)\to(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(1)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence between <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Y,Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Y,Z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math>, assuming that <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Y,Z}\ll P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Y,Z}\ll P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{f}(P_{Y,Z}\parallel P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})=\int q_{T}(y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(x))f\left(\frac{p_{Y,Z}(y,z)}{p_{T}(y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(x))}\right)\nu(x,y,z)dxdydz," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo>∫</mo></mstyle><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{f}(P_{Y,Z}\parallel P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})=\int q_{T}(y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(x))f\left(\frac{p_{Y,Z}(y,z)}{p_{T}(y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(x))}\right)\nu(x,y,z)dxdydz,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where we have assumed that both distributions admit densities <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y,Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y,Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, with respect to some common dominating measure <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, for all choices of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> in the model class.
These divergence measures are also known to admit the following variational representation</p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{f}(P_{Y,Z}\parallel P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})=\sup_{g}\left(\mathbb{E}[g(Y,Z)-f^{*}(g(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)))]\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mi>g</mi></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{f}(P_{Y,Z}\parallel P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})=\sup_{g}\left(\mathbb{E}[g(Y,Z)-f^{*}(g(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)))]\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">which corresponds to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{f}(g,x,y,z,T)=g(y,z)-f^{*}(g(y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{f}(g,x,y,z,T)=g(y,z)-f^{*}(g(y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, the supremum is over all measurable <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{Z}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{Z}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> make the right-hand side (RHS) well-defined (by avoiding <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty-\infty</annotation></semantics></math>). This implies that if we restrict our attention to any smaller class of functions <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, get a lower bound on the divergence, and this gap (that we later refer to as the variational gap) decreases by enlarging the critic class <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. This fact will play a central role in our analysis in the next two subsections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The canonical member of the <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence family is the (forward) relative entropy (or  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">KL</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">KL</abbr>) divergence), with generator function <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(u)=u\log u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(u)=u\log u</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> function in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.1 Variational () ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{KL}(g,x,y,z,T)=\log g(y,z)-g(T(x))+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{KL}(g,x,y,z,T)=\log g(y,z)-g(T(x))+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Other important <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergences are the squared Hellinger divergence with <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(u)=(\sqrt{u}-1)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>u</mi></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(u)=(\sqrt{u}-1)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{H}(g,x,y,z,T)=g(y,z)-g(T(x))/(1-g(T(x)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{H}(g,x,y,z,T)=g(y,z)-g(T(x))/(1-g(T(x)))</annotation></semantics></math>, and the Jensen Shannon divergence with <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(u)=-u(+1)\log((1+u)/2)+u\log u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(u)=-u(+1)\log((1+u)/2)+u\log u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{JS}(g,x,y,z,T)=g(y,z)+\log\left(2-e^{g(T(x))}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{JS}(g,x,y,z,T)=g(y,z)+\log\left(2-e^{g(T(x))}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We now proceed to a discussion of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> defined using a variational objective as introduced in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 3.1 Variational () ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{z}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{z}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{x}}=d_{\mathbf{y}}+d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{x}}=d_{\mathbf{y}}+d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and suppose we have a dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(X_{i},Y_{i},Z_{i})\in\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{Z}:1\leq i\leq n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(X_{i},Y_{i},Z_{i})\in\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{Z}:1\leq i\leq n\}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn i.i.d. from the joint distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{XYZ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{XYZ}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our goal is to learn an invertible neural network (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr>) from a family of homeomorphisms (i.e., each <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is a continuous bijective map with a continuous inverse) using this training dataset. Each candidate <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed into <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathbf{y}}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathbf{y}}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathbf{z}}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathbf{z}}:\mathcal{X}\to\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, and consequently, the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> training process involves population loss functions:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{\mathbf{y}}(T)=\mathbb{E}_{P_{XY}}\left[\|T_{\mathbf{y}}(X)-Y\|_{2}^{2}\right],\quad\text{and}\quad L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)\leq D(P_{Y,Z},P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{\mathbf{y}}(T)=\mathbb{E}_{P_{XY}}\left[\|T_{\mathbf{y}}(X)-Y\|_{2}^{2}\right],\quad\text{and}\quad L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)\leq D(P_{Y,Z},P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(P,Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(P,Q)</annotation></semantics></math> is an appropriate notion of distance or divergence between probability distributions <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q\in\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q\in\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Y}\times\mathcal{Z})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this section, we will focus on the case of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> being an <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence and employ its variational definition stated in (<a href="#S3.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 3.1 Variational () ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) within the empirical risk minimization (ERM) framework.
The use of an “<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>” instead of “<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=</annotation></semantics></math>” when introducing <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> above is a consequence of the variational definition with a restricted critic class as we make precise in <span id="S3.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(INN4)</span> in <a href="#S3.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. More explicitly, we work with a function class <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (the “critic class”), and define <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)=\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{n}}\left\{\mathbb{E}_{P_{YZ}}[g(Y,Z)]-\mathbb{E}_{P_{XY}}[f^{*}(g(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)))]\right\}\leq D_{f}(P_{Y,Z},P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)=\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{n}}\left\{\mathbb{E}_{P_{YZ}}[g(Y,Z)]-\mathbb{E}_{P_{XY}}[f^{*}(g(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)))]\right\}\leq D_{f}(P_{Y,Z},P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">To train an <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> from a family <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> might represent all <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> with a fixed architecture), we use the empirical analogs of the population loss terms,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}(T)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|T_{\mathbf{y}}(X_{i})-Y_{i}\|_{2}^{2},\quad\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{z},n}(T)=\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{n}}\left\{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}g(Y_{i},Z_{i})-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}f^{*}\circ g(Y_{i},T_{\mathbf{z}}(X_{i}))\right\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}(T)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|T_{\mathbf{y}}(X_{i})-Y_{i}\|_{2}^{2},\quad\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{z},n}(T)=\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{n}}\left\{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}g(Y_{i},Z_{i})-\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}f^{*}\circ g(Y_{i},T_{\mathbf{z}}(X_{i}))\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the convex conjugate of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>. The model learned by ERM can then be defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{T}_{n}\in\argmin_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\;\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}(T)+\lambda\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{z},n}(T)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{T}_{n}\in\argmin_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\;\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}(T)+\lambda\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{z},n}(T),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">for some regularization parameter <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. The first loss term (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}</annotation></semantics></math>) forces the learned map <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> from an input <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, while the second loss term (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{z},n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{z},n}</annotation></semantics></math>) ensures that the joint law of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,\hat{T}_{n,\mathbf{z}}(X))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,\hat{T}_{n,\mathbf{z}}(X))</annotation></semantics></math> matches the true joint distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,Z)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, intuitively, if the sample-size <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is large enough, we should expect <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to be a good proxy for <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, an element of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes the population risk:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T^{*}\in\argmin_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\;L_{\mathbf{y}}(T)+\lambda L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T^{*}\in\argmin_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\;L_{\mathbf{y}}(T)+\lambda L_{\mathbf{z}}(T).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.6" class="ltx_p">The performance of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> defined above is governed by two effects: (i) the finite sample effect caused by the uniform deviations between the empirical objectives <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{z},n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{z},n}</annotation></semantics></math> from their population counterparts; and (ii) the variational or approximation error induced by restricting the divergence representation to a class <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. To make the variational loss smaller, we need to increase the capacity of <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, that also causes the uniform deviations between the empirical and population losses to increase. Thus, the crucial challenge is to find the right trade-offs between these two effects to simultaneously drive the overall error to zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the rest of this section, we follow a three-step roadmap. <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">First</em>, we state a general result (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) that obtains an upper bound on the quality of the posterior approximation provided by <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> under a set of high-level assumptions (stated in <a href="#S3.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Second</em>, we present more concrete, verifiable, sufficient conditions for satisfying the requirements of <a href="#S3.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> in <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>. <em id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Finally</em>, we specialize the general theorem to a specific choice of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> architecture (iRes-Nets in <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ 3.2.2 INN Example ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) and critic class (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RKHS</abbr> over truncated domain in <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 3.2. ‣ 3.2.2 INN Example ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>) using Jensen-Shannon divergence in <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS2" title="3.2.2 INN Example ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We now present the assumptions required for stating the main result of this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">To analyze the performance of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> model <math id="S3.Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>), we place the following assumptions:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(INN1): Realizability and Bi-Lipschitz.</span><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
There exists a </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{*}\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{*}\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=T_{\mathbf{y}}^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=T_{\mathbf{y}}^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> almost surely (a.s.) and </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Y,Z}=P_{Y,T^{*}_{z}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Y,Z}=P_{Y,T^{*}_{z}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and furthermore</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\left\{\mathrm{Lip}(T),\,\mathrm{Lip}(T^{-1})\right\}\leq J&lt;\infty." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em">{</mo><mrow><mi>Lip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>Lip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>J</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\left\{\mathrm{Lip}(T),\,\mathrm{Lip}(T^{-1})\right\}\leq J&lt;\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(INN2): Uniform Convergence.</span><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For any confidence level </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, there exist deterministic sequences </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r_{n}\equiv r_{n}(\delta):n\geq 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r_{n}\equiv r_{n}(\delta):n\geq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{n}\equiv u_{n}(\delta):n\geq 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{n}\equiv u_{n}(\delta):n\geq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, with </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{n},u_{n}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{n},u_{n}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, such that the following conditions hold with probability at least </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}|\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}(T)-L_{\mathbf{y}}(T)|\leq u_{n},\quad\text{and}\quad\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}|\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{z},n}(T)-L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)|\leq r_{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}|\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}(T)-L_{\mathbf{y}}(T)|\leq u_{n},\quad\text{and}\quad\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}|\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{z},n}(T)-L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)|\leq r_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(INN3): Moment Bounds.</span><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> There exists an </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, such that the following holds for some </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max\left\{\mathbb{E}[\|(Y,Z)\|^{1+a}],\;\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\mathbb{E}[\|(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)\|^{1+a}]\right\}\leq R." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max\left\{\mathbb{E}[\|(Y,Z)\|^{1+a}],\;\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\mathbb{E}[\|(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)\|^{1+a}]\right\}\leq R.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to denotes the </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> norm, and we use </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|(y,z)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|(y,z)\|</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as a shorthand for </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\|y\|^{2}+\|z\|^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\|y\|^{2}+\|z\|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(INN4): Variational Approximation Gap.</span><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The function class </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contains the </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟎</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> function for all </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and there exists a vanishing deterministic sequence </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{n}\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{n}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}D_{f}(P_{Y,Z}\parallel P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})-L_{z}(T)\leq\eta_{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}D_{f}(P_{Y,Z}\parallel P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)})-L_{z}(T)\leq\eta_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In other words, we assume that the capacity of the critic class </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> grows with </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to approximate the likelihood ratios of all distributions modeled by elements of </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and the true data distribution </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Y,Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Y,Z}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <a href="#S3.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> holds, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the Pinsker-type inequality <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{f}\sqrt{D_{f}(P\parallel Q)}\geq TV(P,Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{f}\sqrt{D_{f}(P\parallel Q)}\geq TV(P,Q)</annotation></semantics></math> for all distributions <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math>, and for some constant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{f}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{f}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, for every measurable <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Y}(A)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Y}(A)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, on the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> probability event of <span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(INN2)</span>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W_{1}\left(P^{(A)}_{X},P^{(A)}_{\hat{T}_{n}^{-1}(Y,Z)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W_{1}\left(P^{(A)}_{X},P^{(A)}_{\hat{T}_{n}^{-1}(Y,Z)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lesssim(r_{n}+u_{n}+\eta_{n})^{\frac{a}{2(1+a)}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≲</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lesssim(r_{n}+u_{n}+\eta_{n})^{\frac{a}{2(1+a)}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-Wasserstein metric, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(A)}_{X}=P_{X|Y\in A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(A)}_{X}=P_{X|Y\in A}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{(A)}_{\hat{T}_{n}^{-1}(Y,Z)}=P_{\hat{T}^{-1}_{n}(Y,Z)|Y\in A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{(A)}_{\hat{T}_{n}^{-1}(Y,Z)}=P_{\hat{T}^{-1}_{n}(Y,Z)|Y\in A}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≲</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math> suppresses the constant factors and lower order terms. The exact expression of the upper bound is in (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of this result is in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>. Note that the Pinsker-type inequality <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TV(P,Q)\leq c_{f}\sqrt{D_{f}(P\parallel Q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TV(P,Q)\leq c_{f}\sqrt{D_{f}(P\parallel Q)}</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied by several divergences, such as relative entropy, Jensen Shannon, Hellinger, and chi-squared divergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The result of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> is obtained by combining two ingredients: (i) using the Pinsker-type inequality, we convert the control over the given <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence into control over total variation, and (ii) a truncation argument (<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) that converts the total variation into a bound on <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> under just finite <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+a)</annotation></semantics></math> moment condition.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmremark1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmremark1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmremark1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">One crucial advantage of our result over <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hagemann and Neumayer (2021, Theorem 2)</a></cite> is that we do not impose the bounded support condition on the random variables <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X,Y,Z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X,Y,Z)</annotation></semantics></math>. The mild <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+a)</annotation></semantics></math> moment requirement significantly broadens the applicability of our result, and in particular, allows us to consider more realistic models used in practice.
Additionally, the dependence on the parameter <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies how the heavy tails affect the approximation quality. We empirically verify this insight in <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS6" title="4.1.6 Effect of moments of 𝑋 on the performance of ‣ 4.1 Effect of design choices on training ‣ 4 Empirical Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.6</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Although Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> characterizes the distance between the true (and unknown) posterior and the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> posterior in terms of <math id="S3.Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> metric, we can use existing inequalities to translate it into other distances. For example, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sriperumbudur et al. (2010, Theorem 21)</a></cite>, we know that <math id="S3.Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{MMD}(P^{(A)}_{X},P^{(A)}_{\hat{T}_{n}^{-1}(Y,Z)})\lesssim W_{1}(P^{(A)}_{X},P^{(A)}_{\hat{T}_{n}^{-1}(Y,Z)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>MMD</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{MMD}(P^{(A)}_{X},P^{(A)}_{\hat{T}_{n}^{-1}(Y,Z)})\lesssim W_{1}(P^{(A)}_{X},P^{(A)}_{\hat{T}_{n}^{-1}(Y,Z)})</annotation></semantics></math> for commonly used kernels, such as the Gaussian and Matérn kernels, and thus <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> also implies a bound on the kernel-MMD distance between the true and estimated posteriors.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A closer look at the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> reveals several reasons why <math id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> metric is the appropriate choice to present the result: (i) By Kantorovich-Rubinstein duality, the <math id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> metric is defined via Lipschitz critic functions. This fact coupled with the assumed bi-Lipschitz structure <span id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(INN1)</span> allows us to transfer bounds from posterior distributions to the forward distributions. (ii) Since <math id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a metric, it satisfies the triangle inequality which leads to a natural decomposition in the proof into terms that correspond directly to the supervised and unsupervised training losses. (iii) The Lipschitz formulation of <math id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly what makes our truncation argument(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) effective for handling heavy tailed distributions under a mild <math id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+a)</annotation></semantics></math> moment assumption.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmremark4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">While stating <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, we only needed to explicitly place a <math id="S3.Thmremark4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+a)</annotation></semantics></math> moment requirement, for some <math id="S3.Thmremark4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. However, since <math id="S3.Thmremark4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of squared losses, the existence of the uniform convergence rates in <span id="S3.Thmremark4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(INN2)</span> implicitly places a stronger <math id="S3.Thmremark4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2+\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2+\beta)</annotation></semantics></math> moment conditions, for some <math id="S3.Thmremark4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. We will make this explicit when identifying verifiable sufficient conditions of these assumptions in <a href="#S3.Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 3.1. ‣ 3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for <a href="#S3.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a></h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> gives us a general result that translates uniform convergence and the variational approximation gap guarantees on the two losses, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathbf{y}}(\hat{T}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathbf{y}}(\hat{T}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathbf{z}}(\hat{T}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathbf{z}}(\hat{T}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> into a bound on the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> metric between the true posterior, and the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> posterior.
We now present more easily verifiable sufficient conditions for the assumptions <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(INN2)-(INN4)</span> required by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, working under the realizability part of the assumption <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(INN1)</span>.
We begin with the usual characterization of uniform convergence in terms of the Rademacher complexities (see <a href="#A1.Thmdefinition1" title="Definition A.1 (Rademacher Complexity). ‣ Appendix A Additional Background ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Additional Background ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>) of the associated function classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that the critic class <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of uniformly bounded functions; that is, <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{n}}\|g\|_{\infty}\leq b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{n}}\|g\|_{\infty}\leq b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, for some <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{n}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{n}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{y}}=\{x\mapsto\langle u,T_{\mathbf{y}}(x)\rangle:T\in\mathcal{T},\,\|u\|\leq 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo rspace="0.059em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{y}}=\{x\mapsto\langle u,T_{\mathbf{y}}(x)\rangle:T\in\mathcal{T},\,\|u\|\leq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and define its Rademacher complexity as</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathfrak{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{y}})=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon^{n},X^{n}}\left[\sup_{f\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{y}}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\epsilon_{i}f(X_{i})\right],\quad\text{with}\quad\epsilon^{n}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle i.i.d.}}{{\sim}}\mathrm{Rademacher}(\{-1,+1\})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">with</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mover><mrow><mi>Rademacher</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathfrak{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{y}})=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon^{n},X^{n}}\left[\sup_{f\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{y}}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\epsilon_{i}f(X_{i})\right],\quad\text{with}\quad\epsilon^{n}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle i.i.d.}}{{\sim}}\mathrm{Rademacher}(\{-1,+1\}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Introduce the two critic class complexities</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathfrak{R}_{n}^{(1)}(\mathcal{G}_{n})=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon^{n},Y^{n},Z^{n}}\left[\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{n}}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\epsilon_{i}g(Y_{i},Z_{i})\right],\quad\mathfrak{R}_{n}^{(2)}(\mathcal{G}_{n},\mathcal{T})=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon^{n},X^{n},Y^{n}}\left[\sup_{T,g}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\epsilon_{i}g(Y_{i},T_{\mathbf{z}}(X_{i}))\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathfrak{R}_{n}^{(1)}(\mathcal{G}_{n})=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon^{n},Y^{n},Z^{n}}\left[\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{n}}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\epsilon_{i}g(Y_{i},Z_{i})\right],\quad\mathfrak{R}_{n}^{(2)}(\mathcal{G}_{n},\mathcal{T})=\mathbb{E}_{\epsilon^{n},X^{n},Y^{n}}\left[\sup_{T,g}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\epsilon_{i}g(Y_{i},T_{\mathbf{z}}(X_{i}))\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{\mathbb{E}[\|Y\|^{2+\beta}],\,\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\mathbb{E}[\|T_{\mathbf{y}}(X)\|^{2+\beta}]\}\leq R_{\mathbf{y}}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.059em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{\mathbb{E}[\|Y\|^{2+\beta}],\,\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\mathbb{E}[\|T_{\mathbf{y}}(X)\|^{2+\beta}]\}\leq R_{\mathbf{y}}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{K_{n}}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}}\to\{y\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}}:\|y\|\leq K_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{K_{n}}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}}\to\{y\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}}:\|y\|\leq K_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the projection on a ball of radius <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, define the projected empirical and population supervised losses:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}^{K_{n}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}^{K_{n}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|\Pi_{K_{n}}(T_{\mathbf{y}}(X_{i}))-\Pi_{K_{n}}(Y_{i})\|^{2},\quad\text{and}\quad L_{\mathbf{y}}^{K_{n}}(T)=\mathbb{E}\left[\|\Pi_{K_{n}}(T_{\mathbf{y}}(X))-\Pi_{K_{n}}(Y)\|^{2}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐲</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\|\Pi_{K_{n}}(T_{\mathbf{y}}(X_{i}))-\Pi_{K_{n}}(Y_{i})\|^{2},\quad\text{and}\quad L_{\mathbf{y}}^{K_{n}}(T)=\mathbb{E}\left[\|\Pi_{K_{n}}(T_{\mathbf{y}}(X))-\Pi_{K_{n}}(Y)\|^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume that the function <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> (in fact, its convex conjugate <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>) satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{1,n}\coloneqq\sup_{|u|\leq b_{n}}|(f^{*})^{\prime}(u)|&lt;\infty,\quad\text{and}\quad A_{2,n}\coloneqq\sup_{|u|\leq b_{n}}|f^{*}(u)|&lt;\infty." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{1,n}\coloneqq\sup_{|u|\leq b_{n}}|(f^{*})^{\prime}(u)|&lt;\infty,\quad\text{and}\quad A_{2,n}\coloneqq\sup_{|u|\leq b_{n}}|f^{*}(u)|&lt;\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, the following two conditions hold with probability at least <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, for a given <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\ in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\ in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}|\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{z},n}(T)-L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}|\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{z},n}(T)-L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E25.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\;\lesssim\;\mathfrak{R}_{n}^{(1)}+A_{1,n}\mathfrak{R}_{n}^{(2)}+(b_{n}+A_{2,n})n^{-1/2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≲</mo><msubsup><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;\lesssim\;\mathfrak{R}_{n}^{(1)}+A_{1,n}\mathfrak{R}_{n}^{(2)}+(b_{n}+A_{2,n})n^{-1/2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}|\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}(T)-L_{\mathbf{y}}(T)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}|\widehat{L}_{\mathbf{y},n}(T)-L_{\mathbf{y}}(T)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E26.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\;\lesssim\;K_{n}\mathfrak{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{y}})+K_{n}^{2}n^{-1/2}+{4R_{\mathbf{y}}}{K_{n}^{-\beta}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≲</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>ℜ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\;\lesssim\;K_{n}\mathfrak{R}_{n}(\mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{y}})+K_{n}^{2}n^{-1/2}+{4R_{\mathbf{y}}}{K_{n}^{-\beta}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lesssim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≲</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lesssim</annotation></semantics></math> suppresses constants that depend on <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\mathbf{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\mathbf{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, a sufficient condition to satisfy <span id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(INN2)</span> with vanishing sequences of <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m17" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{r_{n},u_{n}:n\geq 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r_{n},u_{n}:n\geq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, is to select an appropriate sequence of <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n},b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n},b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to drive both these terms to <math id="S3.Thmproposition1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of (<a href="#S3.E25" title="Equation 25 ‣ Proposition 3.1. ‣ 3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>) relies on some standard arguments from empirical process theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shorack and Wellner, 2009</a>)</cite>, while the justification of (<a href="#S3.E26" title="Equation 26 ‣ Proposition 3.1. ‣ 3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>) requires a combination of symmetrization and vector contraction along with a truncation argument, and we present the details in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we present a simple result stating that the uniform moment bound required in <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(INN3)</span> can be satisfied if the model class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is uniformly Lipschitz. As we will observe in <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS2" title="3.2.2 INN Example ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.2</span></a>, this condition holds for an important class of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmproposition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmproposition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose there exist constants <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0},J&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0},J&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, such that with <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm, we have <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|T(x)\|\leq J_{0}+J\|x\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|T(x)\|\leq J_{0}+J\|x\|</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for all <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, assuming that <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\|X\|^{1+a}]&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\|X\|^{1+a}]&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.Thmproposition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the following uniform moment bounds hold under the realizability assumption:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E27.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max\left\{\mathbb{E}\left[\|(Y,Z)\|^{1+a}\right],\,\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\mathbb{E}\left[\|(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)\|^{1+a}\right]\right\}\leq R,\quad\text{with}\quad R=2^{2a+1}\left(J_{0}^{1+a}+J^{1+a}\mathbb{E}[\|X\|^{1+a}]\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.059em">,</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">with</mtext><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>X</mi><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max\left\{\mathbb{E}\left[\|(Y,Z)\|^{1+a}\right],\,\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\mathbb{E}\left[\|(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)\|^{1+a}\right]\right\}\leq R,\quad\text{with}\quad R=2^{2a+1}\left(J_{0}^{1+a}+J^{1+a}\mathbb{E}[\|X\|^{1+a}]\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since we are working under the realizability assumption, we have <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,Z)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle d}}{{=}}T^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mover><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,Z)\stackrel{{\scriptstyle d}}{{=}}T^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the fact that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x+y)^{p}\leq 2^{p-1}(x^{p}+y^{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x+y)^{p}\leq 2^{p-1}(x^{p}+y^{p})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=1+a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=1+a</annotation></semantics></math>, and the condition that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|T(x)\|\leq J_{0}+J\|x\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|T(x)\|\leq J_{0}+J\|x\|</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\|(Y,Z)\|^{p}\right]\leq 2^{p-1}\left(J_{0}^{p}+J^{p}\mathbb{E}[\|X\|^{p}]\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\|(Y,Z)\|^{p}\right]\leq 2^{p-1}\left(J_{0}^{p}+J^{p}\mathbb{E}[\|X\|^{p}]\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Next, we look at the term <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X))</annotation></semantics></math> and observe that</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X))\|^{p}\leq 2^{p-1}\left(\|Y\|^{p}+\|T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)\|^{p}\right)\leq 2^{p-1}\left(\|(Y,Z)\|^{p}+\|T(X)\|^{p}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X))\|^{p}\leq 2^{p-1}\left(\|Y\|^{p}+\|T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)\|^{p}\right)\leq 2^{p-1}\left(\|(Y,Z)\|^{p}+\|T(X)\|^{p}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">which implies that</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\|(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X))\|^{p}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[\|(Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X))\|^{p}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2^{p-1}\mathbb{E}[\|(Y,Z)\|^{p}]+2^{2(p-1)}\left(J_{0}^{p}+J^{p}\mathbb{E}[\|X\|^{p}]\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2^{p-1}\mathbb{E}[\|(Y,Z)\|^{p}]+2^{2(p-1)}\left(J_{0}^{p}+J^{p}\mathbb{E}[\|X\|^{p}]\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2\times 2^{2(p-1)}\left(J_{0}^{p}+J^{p}\mathbb{E}[\|X\|^{p}]\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>p</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2\times 2^{2(p-1)}\left(J_{0}^{p}+J^{p}\mathbb{E}[\|X\|^{p}]\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Taking the maximum of the two bounds gives us the required expression for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmproposition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmproposition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Introduce the notation <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\equiv P_{YZ}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\equiv P_{YZ}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{T}\equiv P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{T}\equiv P_{Y,T_{\mathbf{z}}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and assume that for all <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\ll Q_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\ll Q_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{*}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{*}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a maximizer in the definition of the population <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), and let <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{g}_{T}=\mathrm{clip}(g^{*}_{T},-b_{n},b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{g}_{T}=\mathrm{clip}(g^{*}_{T},-b_{n},b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> denote its clipped version for <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{n}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{n}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. For some <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{K_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{K_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the ball <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}+d_{\mathbf{z}}}:\|u\|\leq K_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}+d_{\mathbf{z}}}:\|u\|\leq K_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and define the following terms:</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An approximation error term </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{n}\equiv\delta_{n}(b_{n},K_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{n}\equiv\delta_{n}(b_{n},K_{n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> defined as</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\delta_{n}\equiv\delta_{n}(b_{n},K_{n})\coloneqq\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\inf_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{n}}\left\{\mathbb{E}_{P}[|\bar{g}_{T}-g|\bm{1}_{B_{K_{n}}}]+\mathbb{E}_{Q_{T}}[|\bar{g}_{T}-g|\bm{1}_{B_{K_{n}}}]\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≔</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.0835em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.0835em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\delta_{n}\equiv\delta_{n}(b_{n},K_{n})\coloneqq\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\inf_{g\in\mathcal{G}_{n}}\left\{\mathbb{E}_{P}[|\bar{g}_{T}-g|\bm{1}_{B_{K_{n}}}]+\mathbb{E}_{Q_{T}}[|\bar{g}_{T}-g|\bm{1}_{B_{K_{n}}}]\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The tightness term </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}\equiv\tau_{1}(K_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}\equiv\tau_{1}(K_{n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and the clipping error term </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2}\equiv\tau_{2}(b_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2}\equiv\tau_{2}(b_{n})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, as</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tau_{1}(K_{n})=\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}P(B_{K_{n}}^{c})+Q_{T}(B_{K_{n}}^{c})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tau_{1}(K_{n})=\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}P(B_{K_{n}}^{c})+Q_{T}(B_{K_{n}}^{c}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tau_{2}(b_{n})=\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\left\{\mathbb{E}_{P}[(|g_{T}^{*}|-b_{n})^{+}]+A_{1,n}\mathbb{E}_{Q_{T}}\left[(|g_{T}^{*}|-b_{n})^{+}\right]\right\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tau_{2}(b_{n})=\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\left\{\mathbb{E}_{P}[(|g_{T}^{*}|-b_{n})^{+}]+A_{1,n}\mathbb{E}_{Q_{T}}\left[(|g_{T}^{*}|-b_{n})^{+}\right]\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where we use </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a)^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a)^{+}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to denote </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{0,a\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{0,a\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for any </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose there exists a sequence of <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{K_{n},b_{n}:n\geq 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{K_{n},b_{n}:n\geq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\left\{\tau_{2}+A_{1,n}\delta_{n}+(b_{n}+A_{2,n})\,\tau_{1}\right\}=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0em">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\left\{\tau_{2}+A_{1,n}\delta_{n}+(b_{n}+A_{2,n})\,\tau_{1}\right\}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{2,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{2,n}</annotation></semantics></math> were defined in (<a href="#S3.E24" title="Equation 24 ‣ Proposition 3.1. ‣ 3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>).
Then the variational gap <math id="S3.Thmproposition3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> also converges to zero; that is,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{if \eqref{eq:inn-variational-gap-sufficient-condition} is true,}\quad\implies\quad\eta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if (</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#S3.E34" title="Equation 34 ‣ Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a></mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">) is true,</mtext></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{if \eqref{eq:inn-variational-gap-sufficient-condition} is true,}\quad\implies\quad\eta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E35.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\left\{D_{f}(P_{X}\parallel Q_{T})-L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)\right\}\;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle n\to\infty}}{{\longrightarrow}}\;0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em">}</mo></mrow><mover><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mover><mn> 0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sup_{T\in\mathcal{T}}\left\{D_{f}(P_{X}\parallel Q_{T})-L_{\mathbf{z}}(T)\right\}\;\stackrel{{\scriptstyle n\to\infty}}{{\longrightarrow}}\;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of this result is in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>. The structure of (<a href="#S3.E34" title="Equation 34 ‣ Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>) is worth further discussion. The term <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a purely approximation property of the critic class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it enforces the requirement that every optimal critic function can be approximated in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by a bounded element in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with vanishing error. The terms <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can be interpreted as tail approximation terms: <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is controlled by the moment bounds from <span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(INN3)</span>, while <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on how quickly the tails of the optimal critic function decays with the clipping level <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To summarize, the results of this section provide a concrete recipe to apply the more abstract <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> to a particular instantiation of the variational <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> pipeline. In particular, for a chosen model and critic class pair, it suffices to show that the Rademacher complexities of the associated function classes decay sufficiently quickly to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 3.1. ‣ 3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), to establish a uniform bi-Lipschitz property, and through it the moment bounds (<a href="#S3.Thmproposition2" title="Proposition 3.2. ‣ 3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>), and show that the critic class and well-approximate the optimal witness function on high probability regions (<a href="#S3.Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>). In the next subsection, we illustrate these steps for a specific variational <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> architecture with Gaussian <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RKHS</abbr> critic class.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>INN Example</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we instantiate our abstract assumptions and conditions from <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Assumption</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> with a concrete class of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> models and critic function classes. The main goal of this section is demonstrate the the conditions derived in the previous sections are satisfied by a non-trivial, reasonably expressive, and practically relevant pair of model and critic classes. In particular, we show that a residual <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> with bounded weights and biases, trained with a Jensen-Shannon based loss and a Gaussian RKHS critic class, fits into our framework developed in the previous sections.
We begin with a formal description of the model class.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As before, we assume <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{x}}=d_{\mathbf{y}}+d_{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{x}}=d_{\mathbf{y}}+d_{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for an integer <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, define <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T:\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{x}}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T:\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{x}}}\to\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows, with <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{d_{\mathbf{x}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{d_{\mathbf{x}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{x}}\times d_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{x}}\times d_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> identity matrix:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx36" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E36.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle T=(I_{d_{\mathbf{x}}}+F_{M})\circ(I_{d_{\mathbf{x}}}+F_{M-1})\circ\cdots\circ(I_{d_{\mathbf{x}}}+F_{1}),\quad F_{j}(x)\coloneqq W_{j,2}\tanh(W_{j,1}x+b_{j,1})+b_{j,2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mi>tanh</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T=(I_{d_{\mathbf{x}}}+F_{M})\circ(I_{d_{\mathbf{x}}}+F_{M-1})\circ\cdots\circ(I_{d_{\mathbf{x}}}+F_{1}),\quad F_{j}(x)\coloneqq W_{j,2}\tanh(W_{j,1}x+b_{j,1})+b_{j,2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with the following constraints for all <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[M]=\{1,\ldots,M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[M]=\{1,\ldots,M\}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx37" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|W_{j,1}\|_{op}\leq s,\quad\|W_{j,2}\|_{op}\leq s,\quad\max\left\{\|b_{j,1}\|,\|b_{j,2}\|\right\}\leq B." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|W_{j,1}\|_{op}\leq s,\quad\|W_{j,2}\|_{op}\leq s,\quad\max\left\{\|b_{j,1}\|,\|b_{j,2}\|\right\}\leq B.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[M]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[M]</annotation></semantics></math>, the matrices <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{j,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{j,1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{j,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{j,2}</annotation></semantics></math> have dimensions <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{j}\times d_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{j}\times d_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{x}}\times d_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{x}}\times d_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, and let <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\coloneqq\max_{j\in[M]}d_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\coloneqq\max_{j\in[M]}d_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. We split the output coordinates to write <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T(x)=(T_{\mathbf{y}}(x),T_{\mathbf{z}}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T(x)=(T_{\mathbf{y}}(x),T_{\mathbf{z}}(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathbf{y}}(x)\in\mathcal{Y}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathbf{y}}(x)\in\mathcal{Y}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\mathbf{z}}(x)\in\mathcal{Z}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{z}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\mathbf{z}}(x)\in\mathcal{Z}=\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{z}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to verify that this function class is bi-Lipschitz and there exist constants <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{0},J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{0},J</annotation></semantics></math> (depending on <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M,B,s,H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M,B,s,H</annotation></semantics></math>) such that for all <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|T(x)\|\leq J_{0}+J\|x\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|T(x)\|\leq J_{0}+J\|x\|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|T^{-1}(y,z)\|\leq J_{0}+J\|(y,z)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|T^{-1}(y,z)\|\leq J_{0}+J\|(y,z)\|</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This choice of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> model class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> above is driven by the need for a global bi-Lipschitz bounds on both the forward and inverse maps. The residual architecture with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tanh" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>tanh</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tanh</annotation></semantics></math> activations and the norm constraints on the weight and bias terms provide a convenient way of controlling global Lipschitz constants. In contrast the popular coupling-based architectures such as RealNVP of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinh et al. (2014)</a></cite> generally do not admit such global control of Lipschitz constants. We now present the details of our critic class construction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{n}\equiv k_{\gamma_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{n}\equiv k_{\gamma_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Gaussian kernel on <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}+d_{\mathbf{z}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐲</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐳</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d_{\mathbf{y}}+d_{\mathbf{z}}}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx38" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{n}((y,z),(y^{\prime},z^{\prime}))=\exp\left(-\gamma_{n}\|(y,z)-(y^{\prime},z^{\prime})\|^{2}\right)=\exp\left(-\gamma_{n}\left(\|y-y^{\prime}\|^{2}+\|z-z^{\prime}\|^{2}\right)\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{n}((y,z),(y^{\prime},z^{\prime}))=\exp\left(-\gamma_{n}\|(y,z)-(y^{\prime},z^{\prime})\|^{2}\right)=\exp\left(-\gamma_{n}\left(\|y-y^{\prime}\|^{2}+\|z-z^{\prime}\|^{2}\right)\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and let <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{k_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{k_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote its RKHS. Fix a radius parameter <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and a uniform upper bound <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{n}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{n}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and define the critic class as</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx39" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E39.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{G}_{n}=\left\{g=h\bm{1}_{C_{K_{n}}}\;:\;h\in\mathcal{H}_{k_{n}},\;\|h\|_{k_{n}}\leq b_{n}\right\},\quad\text{with}\quad C_{K_{n}}=[-K_{n},K_{n}]^{d_{\mathbf{x}}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.558em">:</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">with</mtext><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{G}_{n}=\left\{g=h\bm{1}_{C_{K_{n}}}\;:\;h\in\mathcal{H}_{k_{n}},\;\|h\|_{k_{n}}\leq b_{n}\right\},\quad\text{with}\quad C_{K_{n}}=[-K_{n},K_{n}]^{d_{\mathbf{x}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Note that <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{0}\in\mathcal{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{0}\in\mathcal{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and each <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathcal{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathcal{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> also satisfies <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|g\|_{\infty}\leq b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|g\|_{\infty}\leq b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> due to the reproducing property and the fact that <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{x}\sqrt{k_{n}(x,x)}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>sup</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{x}\sqrt{k_{n}(x,x)}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This particular choice of the critic class <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen as a compromise between analytical tractability and representation power. The <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RKHS</abbr> associated with Gaussian kernels admit clean bounds on their Rademacher complexity, and are also known to be <em id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">universal</em> in the sense that they are approximate any continuous bounded function over compact domains arbitrarily well in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>sup</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup</annotation></semantics></math> norm. These are the exact properties that we need in <a href="#S3.Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 3.1. ‣ 3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> to ensure uniform convergence, and in <a href="#S3.Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> for controlling the variational approximation gap. The additional truncation on cube <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{K_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{K_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> by the indicator function is a purely technical choice: it ensures the uniform boundedness of the critic class while also decoupling the approximation on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{K_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{K_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> from tail events, which can be handled separately via moment bounds. Furthermore, since the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-ball <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{K_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{K_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> used by <a href="#S3.Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> is contained in <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{K_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{K_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>, any approximation bound <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{K_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{K_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> is also valid on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{K_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{K_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We now present the choice of the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence to be used for the unsupervised loss.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We will employ a Jensen-Shannon divergence based unsupervised loss in our <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> training. This corresponds to <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)=x\log\tfrac{2x}{x+1}+\log\tfrac{2}{x+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)=x\log\tfrac{2x}{x+1}+\log\tfrac{2}{x+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(t)=\log(1+e^{t})-\log 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(t)=\log(1+e^{t})-\log 2</annotation></semantics></math> with</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx40" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{1,n}=\sup_{|t|\leq b_{n}}|(f^{*})^{\prime}(t)|\leq 1,\quad\text{and}\quad A_{2,n}=\sup_{|t|\leq b_{n}}|f^{*}(t)|\leq\log((1+e^{b_{n}})/2)\leq b_{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{1,n}=\sup_{|t|\leq b_{n}}|(f^{*})^{\prime}(t)|\leq 1,\quad\text{and}\quad A_{2,n}=\sup_{|t|\leq b_{n}}|f^{*}(t)|\leq\log((1+e^{b_{n}})/2)\leq b_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Having introduced all the main components, we can now present the main result of this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{G}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{G}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> denote a pair of model and critic classes as introduced in <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ 3.2.2 INN Example ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 3.2. ‣ 3.2.2 INN Example ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> respectively, and define the parameters</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx41" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=M(s\sqrt{H}+B)+\sqrt{d_{\mathbf{x}}}+\sqrt{2\log n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>H</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=M(s\sqrt{H}+B)+\sqrt{d_{\mathbf{x}}}+\sqrt{2\log n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gamma_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gamma_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=K_{n}^{2+\epsilon}\quad\text{for some fixed }\epsilon&gt;0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for some fixed </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=K_{n}^{2+\epsilon}\quad\text{for some fixed }\epsilon&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b_{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E43.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=C_{b}\gamma_{n}^{d_{\mathbf{x}}/4}K_{n}^{2+d_{\mathbf{x}}/2}=C_{b}K_{n}^{2+d_{\mathbf{x}}+\frac{d_{\mathbf{x}}}{4}\epsilon}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=C_{b}\gamma_{n}^{d_{\mathbf{x}}/4}K_{n}^{2+d_{\mathbf{x}}/2}=C_{b}K_{n}^{2+d_{\mathbf{x}}+\frac{d_{\mathbf{x}}}{4}\epsilon}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then, under the realizability assumption <span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(INN1)</span>, and with these choices of the parameters, the ERM model <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{T}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{T}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S3.E14" title="Equation 14 ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx42" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle W_{1}\left(P_{X\mid Y\in A},\,P_{\hat{T}^{-1}_{n}(Y,Z)|Y\in A}\right)=o(1),\quad\text{w.p. at least }1-\delta." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">w.p. at least </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle W_{1}\left(P_{X\mid Y\in A},\,P_{\hat{T}^{-1}_{n}(Y,Z)|Y\in A}\right)=o(1),\quad\text{w.p. at least }1-\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In other words, with these parameters, the models introduced above satisfy the sufficient conditions for <span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(INN2)</span>-<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(INN4)</span> to hold, as derived in <a href="#S3.SS2.SSS1" title="3.2.1 Verifiable Sufficient Conditions for ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of this result is in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned earlier, the purpose of this theorem is to illustrate that our abstract theoretical results are applicable to a non-trivial, reasonably realistic variational <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> pipeline. In particular, <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.2.2 INN Example ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> demonstrates that one can work with architectures that are expressive enough to model high-dimensional data and still verify the technical assumptions required for our posterior accuracy guarantees. Extending our arguments to other classes of invertible architectures, such as coupling-based flows equipped with appropriate spectral or Jacobian regularization, is an interesting direction for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Empirical Results</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first present a series of small-scale probing experiments to understand the effects of various design choices involved in training invertible models (§ <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Effect of design choices on training ‣ 4 Empirical Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), and then apply the insights gained to a practically relevant ocean-acoustic inversion problem (§ <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Geoacoustic Inversion ‣ 4 Empirical Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).
Our objective in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Effect of design choices on training ‣ 4 Empirical Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> is two-fold:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first objective is to provide empirical support for several theoretical claims derived from the results of the previous section on <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> and from <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> on <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">NF</abbr>. In particular, we investigate the use of the variational <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">USL</abbr> framework <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Variational () ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> for training invertible architectures, and we compare the performance of different <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergences with that of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">IPM</abbr>. We further analyze the behavior of the forward and backward training losses when variational <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergences are employed. Additionally, we empirically examine the main theoretical result for <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">NF</abbr> presented in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> by studying the relationship between the number of finite moments of <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance between the true and the estimated distributions. The observed polynomial convergence rates are consistent with our theoretical predictions and analogous to the behavior is established for <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.2 Variational invertible neural networks (V-INN) ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The other direction is empirically explore the effects of certain design choices in practical performance of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr>. This includes the prior loss or reconstruction loss, the effect of dimensionality of the latent space, and role of the support set, and the effect of entropy regularization in Wasserstein metric. This empirical analysis complements the existing literature and providing insights for applied implementations.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The software accompanying this paper is available on GitHub,
<a href="https://github.com/ananya-ac/INN-Project" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://github.com/ananya-ac/INN-Project</a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Effect of design choices on <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> training</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned earlier, in this section, we study the effects of various design choices (such as prior distribution, latent dimension, entropic regularization parameter, etc.) on the training of invertible neural networks.
Before presenting the empirical studies in detail, we first clarify two general aspects. First, unless otherwise stated, all case studies were trained for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> epochs using a batch size of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math>. The model parameters were optimized with the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> regularization. Second, since the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">NLL</abbr> is a widely adopted loss function for training<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dinh et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kruse et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, we incorporate the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">NLL</abbr> in our case studies, complementing the USL described in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Variational () ‣ 3 Main Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> (the relevant definitions are reviewed in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>).
Also, as discussed in <a href="#S2" title="2 Related Works ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, while the Gaussian assumption underlying the computation of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">NLL</abbr> can be restrictive, we avoid such distributional assumptions by leveraging the variational formulation of f-divergences.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1 </span>Effect of prior on training</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this case study, we use the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">IK</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">IK</abbr>) framework described next.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inverse kinematics example:</span>
<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">IK</abbr> is a core problem in robotics, where the objective is to determine the joint configurations required to achieve a desired end-effector position in space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Niku, 2020</a>)</cite>. Efficiently solving this problem is essential for tasks like motion control, path planning, and real-time manipulation. Traditional methods such as analytical solutions and numerical optimization often face challenges as the robot configurations become complicated and number of degrees of freedom (DOF) increases.
Machine learning algorithms are increasingly being employed for solving <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">IK</abbr> problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Toquica et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, with the 4 DOF robot example becoming a standard benchmark in analyzing the analyzing the practical aspect of training invertible architecture
<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ardizzone et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kruse et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hagemann and Neumayer, 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We consider an articulated robotic arm that moves vertically along a rail and rotates at three joints.
These four degrees of freedom constitute the parameter vector
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=[X^{(1)},X^{(2)},X^{(3)},X^{(4)}]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=[X^{(1)},X^{(2)},X^{(3)},X^{(4)}]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The dataset is generated using Gaussian priors defined as
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{(i)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{(i)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where the standard deviations are specified as
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{1}=0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{1}=0.25</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{2}=\sigma_{3}=\sigma_{4}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{2}=\sigma_{3}=\sigma_{4}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the forward kinematic process of the robotics arm is modeled as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx43" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=X^{(1)}+l_{1}\sin(X^{(2)})+l_{2}\sin(X^{(3)}-X^{(2)})+l_{3}\sin(X^{(4)}-X^{(2)}-X^{(3)});" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=X^{(1)}+l_{1}\sin(X^{(2)})+l_{2}\sin(X^{(3)}-X^{(2)})+l_{3}\sin(X^{(4)}-X^{(2)}-X^{(3)});</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E46.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=l_{1}\cos(X^{(2)})+l_{2}\cos(X^{(3)}-X^{(2)})+l_{3}\cos(X^{(4)}-X^{(2)}-X^{(3)})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=l_{1}\cos(X^{(2)})+l_{2}\cos(X^{(3)}-X^{(2)})+l_{3}\cos(X^{(4)}-X^{(2)}-X^{(3)}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the arm segment lengths are given by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{1}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{1}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{2}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{2}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{3}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{3}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We trained an <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> to model the relationship between the joint angles of a robotic arm and a point in its 2D workspace. All models were trained using the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">NLL</abbr> loss formulation for the INN. The input consisted of 4 variables corresponding to the joint angles of the robotic arm, and the output was a 2-dimensional vector representing a point in the 2D workspace.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For this case study, we use two different regularization priors: a Gaussian prior and a uniform prior.
The Gaussian prior represents the correct prior (Normal prior with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu=[0,0,0,0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu=[0,0,0,0]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma=[0.25,0.5,0.5,0.5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma=[0.25,0.5,0.5,0.5]</annotation></semantics></math>) as the data-generation process follows the same Gaussian distribution. The corresponding prior loss can be computed using the negative log-likelihood, up to a constant, as follows</p>
<table id="S4.EGx44" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\prescript{p}{}{L_{x}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(T^{-1}(Y_{i},Z_{i})-\tilde{X}_{i})^{2}\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>L</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\prescript{p}{}{L_{x}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}(T^{-1}(Y_{i},Z_{i})-\tilde{X}_{i})^{2}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\tilde{X}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\tilde{X}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> is the data generated from the input.
A uniform prior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andrle et al., 2021</a>)</cite> also represents an inconsistent with the true distribution. If the input variable ranges from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b&gt;a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b&gt;a</annotation></semantics></math>), then the loss term is as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx45" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\prescript{p}{}{L_{x}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\max(0,T^{-1}(Y_{i},Z_{i})-b)+\max(0,a-T^{-1}(Y_{i},Z_{i}))\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>L</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\prescript{p}{}{L_{x}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\max(0,T^{-1}(Y_{i},Z_{i})-b)+\max(0,a-T^{-1}(Y_{i},Z_{i}))\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20480v1/figures/prior_obs/NLL.png" id="S4.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">No prior</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20480v1/figures/prior_obs/NLL_normal_1.png" id="S4.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Correct Prior,
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S4.F2.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20480v1/figures/prior_obs/NLL_uniform.png" id="S4.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center" style="font-size:90%;">Misspecified Prior,
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S4.F2.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20480v1/figures/prior_obs/NLL_normal.png" id="S4.F2.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/476;" width="476" height="476" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Correct Prior,
<br class="ltx_break"><math id="S4.F2.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}=100</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
The four plots illustrate the results of the inverse kinematics case study with different prior regularizations.
(a) shows samples from a model trained without any prior loss (i.e., with prior weight <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math>). (b) and (c) display samples from models trained with a prior weight of <math id="S4.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. In (b), the model assumes a Gaussian prior over the joint configuration, resulting in a scaled <math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> loss between the ground truth and the generated samples. In contrast, (c) uses a uniform prior <math id="S4.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is inconsistent with the true distribution. (d) illustrates results from a model trained with a Gaussian prior, using <math id="S4.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}=100</annotation></semantics></math>. The cross indicates the true end-effector position. All models were trained using the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">NLL</abbr> loss formulation for the INN.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
