" height="11.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="68.74"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.pic2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.pic2.2" class="ltx_text">-Distance</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 29.61 239.22)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:19.15em;--ltx-fo-height:0.61em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.17em;font-size:7.97pt;" height="8.61" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="211.21"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.pic2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">Effect of Entropy Regularization parameter</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#CCCCCC;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="0.8" stroke="#CCCCCC" stroke-opacity="1"><path d="M 159.87 180.8 h 104.28 v 38.59 h -104.28 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#CCCCCC;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="0.8" stroke="#CCCCCC" stroke-opacity="1" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 164.02 191.83)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 33.05)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 8.26)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 18.73 0) translate(-0.28,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 3.04 -3.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.94em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="26.91"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.pic2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 70.18 0) translate(-0.28,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 3.04 -3.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.44em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="19.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.pic2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 24.79)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 18.73 0) translate(-0.28,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 3.04 -3.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.pic2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 70.18 0) translate(-0.28,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 3.04 -3.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="13.84"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.pic2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S4.F6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">This diagram illustrates the effect of entropy regularization on the Wasserstein distance. The circular dots represent the debiased Sinkhorn divergence, while the hollow squares indicate the Sinkhorn approximation of the
<math id="S4.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> distance. The x-axis shows training time and the y-axis shows the <math id="S4.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-distance between true and generated end-effector positions.
</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the impact of latent dimension size on training and inference, we train models with different latent dimensions (which can be tuned by appropriately changing <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> by padding). The loss functions employed are the Sinkhorn divergence and the variational formulation of the forward <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">KL</abbr> divergence.
Also, similar to previous examples, the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">IK</abbr> problem described in Section <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS1" title="4.1.1 Effect of prior on training ‣ 4.1 Effect of design choices on training ‣ 4 Empirical Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.1</span></a> is used for this case study.
As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.1.3 Effect of latent dimension in invertible neural networks ‣ 4.1 Effect of design choices on training ‣ 4 Empirical Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we observe an
approximately local <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>-shaped relationship between latent dimension and inference performance, where
the overall trend is multimodal.
Additionally, training with the Sinkhorn divergence exhibits greater stability compared to the forward <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">KL</abbr> divergence.
While in this case study we focus on the dimension of latent variable, the work in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hagemann and Neumayer (2021)</a></cite> investigated the number of modes of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating that parameterizing <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> in dependence on the labels <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is helpful for obtaining stable <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">INN</abbr> with reasonable Lipschitz constants.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.4 </span>Effect of entropic regularization on Sinkhorn divergence and Wasserstein distance approximation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate the effect of entropic regularization on the debiased Sinkhorn divergence and the Sinkhorn approximation of the Wasserstein distance, we vary the entropic regularization parameter across experiments. The model is trained for the inverse kinematics problem same training configuration described in Section <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS1" title="4.1.1 Effect of prior on training ‣ 4.1 Effect of design choices on training ‣ 4 Empirical Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1.1</span></a>. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.1.3 Effect of latent dimension in invertible neural networks ‣ 4.1 Effect of design choices on training ‣ 4 Empirical Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, a decrease in entropic regularization tends to improves sample quality but increases training time. Additionally, training with the Sinkhorn divergence exhibits greater stability compared to the Sinkhorn approximation of the Wasserstein distance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.5 </span>Effect of support set of the latent distribution and the data distribution</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate the effect of the support set of the latent distribution when training with the variational formulation of the KL divergence and the Sinkhorn divergence. In this experiment, the data distribution is fixed as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The latent distributions considered for comparison are <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(3,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(3,4)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(5,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(5,6)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(10,11)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(10,11)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}(15,16)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}(15,16)</annotation></semantics></math>.
As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4.1.5 Effect of support set of the latent distribution and the data distribution ‣ 4.1 Effect of design choices on training ‣ 4 Empirical Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1.5 Effect of support set of the latent distribution and the data distribution ‣ 4.1 Effect of design choices on training ‣ 4 Empirical Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the quality of the generated samples deteriorates as the support of the latent distribution becomes increasingly disjoint from that of the true distribution. This effect is observed for both cost functions; however, the model trained using the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>-divergence formulation of the KL divergence exhibits a more pronounced degradation compared to the model trained with the Sinkhorn divergence.
Thus, our empirical results support the hypothesis that models trained with the Sinkhorn divergence are more robust to mismatches between the supports of the latent and prior distributions.</p>
</div>
