</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.6 </span>Effect of moments of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> on the performance of <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">NF</abbr></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we investigate how the number of finite moments of the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> affects the performance of the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">NF</abbr>. To ensure that the moment of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> can be adjusted, we assumed <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has a Pareto distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arnold, 2014</a>)</cite>. That is, the dataset was generated by sampling from a Pareto distribution characterized by a shape parameter <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and a scale parameter <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case the probability density function of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S4.E54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X;x_{m},\alpha)=\begin{cases}\dfrac{\alpha x_{m}^{\alpha}}{X^{\alpha+1}},&amp;X\geq x_{m},\\
0,&amp;X&lt;x_{m},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi></msubsup></mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X;x_{m},\alpha)=\begin{cases}\dfrac{\alpha x_{m}^{\alpha}}{X^{\alpha+1}},&amp;X\geq x_{m},\\
0,&amp;X&lt;x_{m},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{m}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{m}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">NF</abbr> trained with the variational formulation of KL-Divergence. The latent space was defined by a <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional random vector drawn from a standard normal distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here, the parameter <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is varied from 1 to 10, and, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.1.6 Effect of moments of 𝑋 on the performance of ‣ 4.1 Effect of design choices on training ‣ 4 Empirical Results ‣ VINA: Variational Invertible Neural Architectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, the Wasserstein-1 distance between the true and generated distributions decreased. This is consistent with our theoretical results in <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> that as the number of finite moment of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance between the true and estimated distributions decreases.</p>
</div>
